WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.390
A sedimentary environment is a place where material is produced, moved

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.090
or deposited. Steep mountain slopes release large angular blocks.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:12.130
Mountain streams can carry coarse sediment through bedrock canyons, and channels

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.970
may split and rejoin as braided streams beyond the mountains.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.030
Fluvial describes river processes and deposits;

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.910
it does not name one grain size.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.530
On gentler terrain, a meandering stream is bordered by a floodplain.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.910
Floodwater spreads mud and sand across that surface.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.390
At a river mouth, the current enters standing lake or ocean

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:35.670
water and slows. Sediment accumulates into a delta that builds outward.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.230
A floodplain lies alongside a channel;

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:39.910
a delta grows at its mouth.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.110
Wind transports sand and finer particles.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.250
Finer material can travel onward, leaving dunes composed mainly of well-sorted

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.170
sand. A glacier instead carries a wide mixture, from ground rock

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:56.370
flour to large blocks. Melting releases both water and sediment.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.110
Different agents can therefore produce quite different deposits from the same

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:00.830
mountain source.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:05.890
A lake contains several environments, from wave-worked shorelines to quieter central

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.470
water. The shoreline may be sandy or rocky depending on the

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:14.190
material available. In wetlands, saturated ground supports abundant vegetation.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.490
Water can limit exposure to oxygen, helping plant material become an

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.430
important part of the accumulating sediment.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:26.430
Topography, water and local geology control which environments develop.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:31.210
To interpret an ancient deposit, combine grain size, sorting, structures and

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.410
biological remains. Angular blocks beside a cliff support a slope setting;

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.290
rounded gravel in a channel supports stream transport.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:42.430
One sand layer alone cannot distinguish a desert, river or beach.
